{"t":1788648879,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"a9583526-800c-475b-8dd5-866b6bbd5b57","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-05T13:33:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 051333\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n933 AM EDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nShowers and storms have made it to western portions of the\nforecast area, with the timeline accelerating a bit compared to\nthe previous forecast. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Shower and thunderstorm chances remain today as the front\n  slowly sags south through region. Marginal to Slight Risk of\n  severe storms for most of VA and eastern WV.\n\n- 2) Drier conditions with seasonable temperatures and lower \n  humidity Sunday into early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Shower and thunderstorm chances remain today as the front \nslowly sags south through region. Marginal to Slight Risk of severe\nstorms for most of VA and eastern WV.\n\nThe MCS that we had been tracking in the OH Valley earlier this\nmorning has arrived into our western areas a couple hours\nearlier than expected, as the system sped up this morning.\nSeeing a few 40+ mph gusts in the strongest cores so far this\nmorning, so nothing severe at this time, but that threat does\nstill exist into the afternoon. This MCS is moving into an\nenvironment with at least modest CAPE values around 1000 J/kg\nand wind shear around 20-30 knots. It isn't the most favorable\nenvironment for organized severe weather, but there is just\nenough of a combination of ingredients to yield a non-zero\nsevere threat through the afternoon, hence the marginal risk\n(level 1 out of 5) from SPC. The slight does clip Nelson county,\nbut is focused primarily south. This checks out, as the\nenvironment will become increasingly favorable for some more\norganized damaging gusts the further south and east you go,\nwhere there is more instability. For storms that do manage to\nget severe today, damaging winds will be the main threat, along\nwith frequent lightning and locally heavy rain rates. Areas \nnorth of I-66 should stay clear of the severe threat today, \nthough a pop up shower or storm can't be ruled out into the \nearly afternoon hours. \n\nHighs today will range from the upper 70s and low 80s along the \nPA/MD line to around 90 just south of Charlottesville, VA. Lows \ntonight will fall into the upper 50s and mid 60s as drier air \npunches into the region. Humidity will slowly lower throughout \nthe day as the front pushes south. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier conditions with seasonable temperatures and \nlower humidity Sunday into early next week.\n\nBroad high pressure over the Great Lakes will track over the \nMid-Atlantic Sunday into Monday, with accompanying mid-level \ndry air that will bring down humidity and t-storm chances. Daily\nhighs drop into the low 80s, along with drier dewpoints in the \nmid to upper 50s. Overnight lows in the 50s and low 60s.\n\nSurface high pressure begins to transition south of the Mid-Atlantic \non Tuesday as upper level ridging builds over the Southeast. \nTemperatures likely begin to creep up again during this time, \naccompanied by increasing humidity as mid-level dry air tracks south \nof the region with more humid air coming in from the north. This \nwill correspond with strong upper-level trough forming over Quebec \naround midweek, with an accompanying cold front tracking across the \nEast Coast sometime between Wednesday and Thursday. This could \ncreate some chances for precipitation over the region, with some \nindicators for low-end instability over the area as this mid-level \nmoisture tracks southeast. Severe probabilities look low at this \ntime, but a few thunderstorms are not completely out of the question \nas this front moves through.\n\nLooking towards late next week, temperatures likely drop again in \nthe wake of this cold front Friday as it tracks to the south.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /13Z SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nSped up the timing for storms at CHO today given recent radar\ntrends with the MCS moving into the area out of the OH valley.\nVFR conditions are still expected to prevail for the most part,\nbut any storms will result in VSBY reductions. Additionally, for\nthe metro terminals, have removed the PROB30s from earlier, as\nthe storm threat should stay southwest of the terminals. While a\nstorm cannot be ruled out, chances have dropped closer to 20\npercent, with highest confidence southwest. \n\nPrevailing VFR conditions are anticipated at terminals Sunday \nthrough Wednesday. Easterly winds shift southerly on Tuesday \nmorning. Next chance for thunderstorms arrives with a cold front \nlate next week.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nNo marine hazards are expected through the holiday weekend. \nGenerally light flow is expected, though onshore gusts could \napproach marginal SCA levels late Sunday afternoon.\n\nNortheasterly winds Monday shift southerly on Tuesday. Winds \nstay below 15 knots through Monday and Tuesday. Winds could \nincrease to SCA levels on Wednesday ahead of an incoming cold \nfront.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...CS/CJL/SRT\nAVIATION...CS/CJL/SRT\nMARINE...CS/CJL/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1788648850,"days":{"2026-09-05":{"hi":null,"lo":67,"pop":8,"short":"Partly Cloudy"},"2026-09-06":{"hi":84,"lo":64,"pop":6,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-07":{"hi":84,"lo":63,"pop":2,"short":"Patchy Fog then Sunny"},"2026-09-08":{"hi":86,"lo":65,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-09":{"hi":89,"lo":73,"pop":2,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-10":{"hi":91,"lo":68,"pop":32,"short":"Mostly Sunny then Slight Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-11":{"hi":83,"lo":63,"pop":16,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers then Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-12":{"hi":84,"lo":n